.TH GETTY 8
.SH NAME
getty  \- set typewriter mode and read a login name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/getty
[
.I char
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Getty
is invoked
by
.IR  init (8)
immediately after a typewriter is opened.
It reads the user's login name and calls
.IR login (1)
with the name as argument.
.PP
.I Init
calls
.I getty
with a single character argument taken from
the
.IR ttys (5)
file entry for the terminal line.
That argument selects an entry of a table built into
.IR getty ,
giving the modes the line is set to while the name is read,
the modes it is left in for
.IR login ,
and the `login:' greeting message.
An argument naming no entry selects the first.
.PP
The user's name is terminated by a new-line or
carriage-return character.
In the second case CRMOD mode is set
(see
.IR  ioctl (2)).
.PP
The name is scanned to see if
it contains any lower-case alphabetic characters; if not,
and if the name is nonempty, the
system is told to map any future upper-case characters
into the corresponding lower-case characters.
.PP
Finally,
.I login
is called with the user's name as argument.
.SH "THIS MACHINE"
.B Note:
the table has ONE entry, `0', and it describes the Consul-254 typewriter,
which is the only kind of terminal this machine has.
The line is read RAW and handed to
.I login
as ECHO|CRMOD|XTABS, which is what the driver
.RI ( sc (4),
kernel/dev/sc.c) sets on a first open.
.PP
v7's table had thirteen entries of PDP-11 baud rates, and none of it
survives, because none of it describes a parallel character-at-a-time
typewriter:
.TP
speeds
There is no baud rate.
.I Sg_ispeed
and
.I sg_ospeed
are stored by
.IR ioctl (2)
and read by nothing, so v7's `3' entry \- which probed the connect
speed and chose a table from it \- could only ever have read back B0.
The
.B break
key, which in v7 made
.I getty
cycle to the next speed in the entry's chain, has nothing to cycle to
and no way to be pressed.
.TP
delays
The CR, NL, TAB and FF delay bits are honoured by this kernel: they
queue a delay byte that the driver turns into a
.IR timeout (9).
They exist for a carriage that needs time to return, which this one
does not, so no entry here sets them.
.TP
parity
The driver sends and receives seven bits, so ANYP, EVENP and ODDP
describe nothing.
v7's
.I getty
also ORed an even-parity bit into every character it printed, from a
128-byte table; that table is gone.
.TP
LCASE
The Consul's own character code has no lower-case Latin letters, so the
line runs in ASCII and the driver does not fold case.
A
.I getty
that set LCASE would undo that.
.SH FILES
/etc/ttys
.SH "SEE ALSO"
init(8), login(1), ioctl(2), ttys(5)
